WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.843
A supplier brochure, design calculation
and executed test record are

2
00:00:04.963 --> 00:00:06.541
different evidence types.

3
00:00:06.661 --> 00:00:09.183
Start with the question you need to
answer.

4
00:00:09.303 --> 00:00:13.612
A generic brochure may describe a product
family without identifying

5
00:00:13.732 --> 00:00:15.631
the purchased configuration.

6
00:00:15.751 --> 00:00:20.423
A calculation may rely on conditions that
do not apply to our process.

7
00:00:20.543 --> 00:00:24.596
An executed record needs traceable results
and a clear method.

8
00:00:24.716 --> 00:00:28.791
More pages do not necessarily provide more
relevant evidence.

9
00:00:29.411 --> 00:00:33.933
Where third-party validation protocols are
used, site personnel should check

10
00:00:34.053 --> 00:00:38.640
their suitability and alignment with
internal procedures before approval.

11
00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.789
In our example, the vendor protocol omits
the

12
00:00:41.909 --> 00:00:44.000
required sensor-failure challenge.

13
00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.621
Record the gap and arrange a justified
supplement rather than accepting

14
00:00:48.741 --> 00:00:50.064
a complete stamp.

15
00:00:50.184 --> 00:00:55.496
DQ can identify this future verification
need while supplier documentation

16
00:00:55.616 --> 00:00:57.173
is still being developed.

17
00:00:57.293 --> 00:01:01.709
Site responsibility does not disappear
because a specialist supplier

18
00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:03.173
prepared the template.

19
00:01:03.793 --> 00:01:08.444
Factory acceptance testing can provide
useful evidence, but it is not a

20
00:01:08.564 --> 00:01:10.548
replacement label for DQ.

21
00:01:10.668 --> 00:01:15.084
Any planned reuse of appropriate factory
tests needs a documented

22
00:01:15.204 --> 00:01:19.876
justification that considers the
applicable configuration and whether

23
00:01:19.996 --> 00:01:23.132
transport or installation can affect the
function.

24
00:01:23.252 --> 00:01:25.590
Some checks may still be needed on site.

25
00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:29.934
For example, a factory record cannot
demonstrate that the site’s final

26
00:01:30.054 --> 00:01:32.081
utility connection is correct.

27
00:01:32.201 --> 00:01:35.604
Keep the evidence claim within what was
actually examined.

28
00:01:36.224 --> 00:01:40.534
The factory report refers to controller
version one, while the supplied

29
00:01:40.654 --> 00:01:42.638
design proposes version two.

30
00:01:42.758 --> 00:01:46.619
Do not automatically accept everything or
discard everything.

31
00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:51.816
Identify what changed, assess effects on
relevant functions and decide which

32
00:01:51.936 --> 00:01:53.792
evidence remains applicable.

33
00:01:53.912 --> 00:01:56.622
Document the rationale and additional
work.

34
00:01:56.742 --> 00:02:00.539
This focused review is more defensible
than deciding from the

35
00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:02.152
version number alone.

36
00:02:02.272 --> 00:02:05.984
The same reasoning applies to substituted
instruments, valves

37
00:02:06.104 --> 00:02:07.982
and software configurations.
